tion took the helios  clinic  by surprise  as far as testing capacities are  concerned in terms of masks and  protective clothing  we are prepared we know where and when  to hospitalize patients  we can make quick diagnoses and quick  decisions  everyone who arrives at the hospital is  tested for coronavirus before they're  admitted  and patients are strictly separated  those who test negative  go to the green ward those who test  positive  go to the red ward and suspected cases  go to the yellow ward  bruno tegel feels this system is safe  we haven't seen a single case of  patients infecting staff  since these measures were initiated now  treating coronavirus patients  has become routine  the applause once heard from german  balconies has quietened down  people seem to have forgotten the  significance of health care workers  that so many coronavirus deniers have  taken to the streets in recent weeks  is something nurses and doctors struggle  to understand  some people say i've never seen  coronavirus so i don't believe in it  well i've never seen electricity but i  don't put my finger in the socket  we've seen coronavirus here we've seen  death  bruno tegel has also seen the effects of  the coronavirus  first hand but he knows that his  hospital is well prepared  for what the pandemic might bring well  monica gandhi is an infectious disease  doctor and researcher with the  university of california san francisco  monica great to have you on the show  what do the rising rates new lockdowns  and  latest preparations all tell you  you know we are either in a second wave  or more likely the continuation of our  first wave and unfortunately  we haven't gotten on top of this covid  pandemic  the cases are still rising and they've  been rising in germany and throughout  the united states  and in europe however death rates are  staying lower than they were during the  first wave  what do you call lockdowns though a  blunt instrument in the fight against  the coronavirus  be